From: Stanislavs Nilovs <valorcool@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 21:36:15 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: hci_qca: Fix WCN3950 missing regulator release

hci_qca driver is missing release of power regulators when module is
powered off or serial device is removed. This issue is most noticeable
during system shutdown, since the regulator core is firing a warning
backtrace that regulator to be freed still has enable_count.

The issue was found with the help of Claude Opus 5 (Anthropic), which
was asked to review drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c for the appearing
regulator warning backtrace during system shutdown. The fix itself was
generated by the tool and reviewed by me by applying it, building the
code and verifying issue is not reproduced anymore.

Tested on real hardware, but not on Linux's master branch. Driver was
backported to Linux 5.15 for internal testing and this is when the issue
was noticed.

Fixes: d5712c511cb3 ("Bluetooth: qca: add WCN3950 support")
Signed-off-by: Stanislavs Nilovs <valorcool@gmail.com>
Acked-by: Bartosz Golaszewski <bartosz.golaszewski@oss.qualcomm.com>
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/20261027193615.1930624-1-valorcool@gmail.com
---
BACKPORT of the posting (linux-bluetooth, patchwork 14919536, state New on
2026-11-17; not in mainline 63fdb511c27a or next-20261116), with the context
of our stack (the remove switch also lists WCN7860).

Until 2026-11-17 (kernel audit v3) this file was ROCKNIX SM6115 0024,
"include WCN3950 in WCN family switches": the same three lines and two probe
hunks more, for the flow without a power sequencer. One counted a WCN3950
without a BT_EN GPIO as not power-controllable, so it was never powered off;
the other made a failed SW_CTRL lookup fatal. Both are dropped:
  - No board here reaches them. The Air X, RG 55G1 and RP Classic have a
    qcom,wcn3950-pmu node and no enable-gpios, so probe takes the sequencer.
  - The vendor powers this chip off through its rails with no enable or
    reset GPIO, and treats SW_CTRL as optional (bluetooth-power.c; the stock
    Air X node is qca,wcn3990 with a SW_CTRL GPIO and four supplies only).
  - Mainline does the same for the WCN399x family, and nobody posted either
    hunk.
With a sequencer only the first hunk runs: the power-off pulse before
pwrseq_disable().

 drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
@@ -2322,6 +2322,7 @@ static void qca_power_off(struct hci_uart *hu)
 	power = qcadev->bt_power;

 	switch (soc_type) {
+	case QCA_WCN3950:
 	case QCA_WCN3988:
 	case QCA_WCN3990:
 	case QCA_WCN3991:
@@ -2346,6 +2347,7 @@ static void qca_power_off(struct hci_uart *hu)
         }

 	switch (soc_type) {
+	case QCA_WCN3950:
 	case QCA_WCN3988:
 	case QCA_WCN3990:
 	case QCA_WCN3991:
@@ -2673,6 +2675,7 @@ static void qca_serdev_remove(struct serdev_device *serdev)
 	struct qca_power *power = qcadev->bt_power;

 	switch (qcadev->btsoc_type) {
+	case QCA_WCN3950:
 	case QCA_WCN3988:
 	case QCA_WCN3990:
 	case QCA_WCN3991:
